WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.020
Bear's Notes
One Electron Interferes With Itself

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:09.443
Fire electrons at two slits so slowly that only one is ever in the machine — each arrives as a single dot.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.243
Ten electrons in, the dots look like random specks — no pattern at all.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.507
Common sense says they should pile into two heaps, one behind each slit.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.550
Keep firing, and something impossible begins to show.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.827
Ripples passing through two openings overlap — crest on crest brightens, crest on trough cancels.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:33.270
So a wave hitting two slits paints stripes on the far screen: strong, weak, strong.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.223
A ball can't overlap with anything: it takes one slit or the other.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.363
Balls through two slits stack into exactly two heaps — nothing in between, no stripes.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.013
Now watch the real electrons: two hundred dots, and a faint clustering appears.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.200
Six thousand dots, and unmistakable stripes are forming.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.743
Seventy thousand dots: clean bright and dark bands — a full interference pattern.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.427
Lay the wave's stripe pattern over the dots, and it fits perfectly.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:03.917
And there are bands where electrons never land — forbidden zones no two-heap story can explain.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.160
Remember: the electrons flew alone — there was never a second electron to interfere with.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.733
So each electron's wave passes through both slits at once and interferes with itself.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.380
The dot is just where the electron is found; the stripes map where finding it is likely.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:24.127
That likelihood map has a name: psi squared, the square of the electron's wave function.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:30.717
And the stripe spacing is set by the electron's wavelength — lambda equals h over p, de Broglie's rule.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:35.857
One electron arrives like a particle and travels like a wave — both, every single time.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.947
Peek at which slit it took, and the stripes vanish — not because you jostled it, but because you know.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:45.930
This isn't just electrons: molecules of two thousand atoms have painted the same stripes.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:53.450
You have a wavelength too — walking, about ten to the minus thirty-five meters, far too small for any doorway to reveal.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:02:00.287
We didn't say what the wave function actually is, or how crystals first revealed matter waves — each is its own video.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:06.240
Try it yourself: compute your own de Broglie wavelength, h over m times v, on your next walk.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:12.953
Thanks for watching One Electron Interferes With Itself — more at youtube dot com, slash, at Nik Bear Brown.
